<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ingavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ingavi was fought on 18 November 1841 near Viacha, south-west of La Paz, between the Bolivian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-ballivian">José Ballivián</a> and an invading Peruvian army led by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a>. The Bolivians won a decisive victory and Gamarra was killed, ending Peruvian attempts to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. The battle secured Bolivian independence and became a lasting symbol of Bolivian nationhood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ingavi.jpg" alt="Battle of Ingavi" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_d%27Ingavi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Ingavi was the decisive engagement of the Peruvian–Bolivian War of 1841–1842. After the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>–Bolivian Confederation, the Peruvian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a> decided to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, using the political chaos there as a pretext. Gamarra had supported a union of the two countries from the beginning, but wanted one completely dominated by the Peruvian government rather than a confederation between two nations.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> then had three rival governments: a legitimate one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a> under José Mariano Serrano, another at Cochabamba under José Miguel de Velasco, and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-ballivian">José Ballivián</a>, who had proclaimed himself president at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a>. Faced with the danger of a Peruvian invasion, the three joined under Ballivián and readied their armies, which repelled the Peruvians at Ingavi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With Gamarra dead, the Peruvian army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. The news caused chaos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, where Vice President Manuel Menéndez struggled to keep his authority. He was soon deposed, and Juan Crisóstomo Torrico took power and restored order. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s last attempt to take control of Bolivia.</p><p>The Bolivian army followed its victory by invading southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, but did not have enough troops to hold it. On 7 January 1842, in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a>, Peruvian montoneros raised at Iquique by Major Juan Buendía defeated a detachment under Colonel José María García, who was killed. Bolivian troops left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> and Tarapacá in February 1842 and fell back on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puno">Puno</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a>, Peruvian militias expelled the Bolivian troops who had sought to take over the port. In the battles of Motoni and Orurillo Peru drove out the remaining Bolivian forces on its territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> in turn faced the threat of invasion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ingavi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1841 José Ballivián defeated a Peruvian invasion at the <b>Battle of Ingavi</b>, where the Peruvian president Agustín Gamarra was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Gamarra was killed invading Bolivia at the <b>Battle of Ingavi</b> in 1841.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
